**Quality requirements for task.md:**

- **Expected Files are MANDATORY for every task that creates or modifies code.**
  Declare one backticked repo-relative path per line under `**Expected Files:**`.
  That list is the task's write scope and the boundary `spec-implement` enforces.
  A recognized **Validation** command is ADDITIONAL proof — it is never a
  substitute for the declared paths. A non-documental task without Expected
  Files is rejected (`spec-new task-verifiability`) before implement.
  Documentation or analysis tasks MUST name their document path in Expected
  Files as well (e.g. `docs/usage.md`).
- **Existing tests are part of the scope.** Before writing `task.md`, find every
  existing test that imports a module this spec changes (search `tests/` and
  `src/tests/`) and declare that test file in the Expected Files of the task that
  changes the module. The finalizer rejects a spec whose changed module is
  imported by an undeclared existing test (`spec-new existing-test-coverage`).
- **Declare the spec test scope.** `requirements.md` MUST cite at least one spec
  test file in the project's own convention (`.test.js`, `.test.ts`, `.test.tsx`,
  `.test.mjs`, `test_*.py`, `*_test.go`, `*Tests.cs`, …) or carry
  `no-test-scope: <justification>`.
  Without it the finalizer rejects the spec with `No test scope declared` and the
  spec can never reach audit.
- Tasks must be small enough that each can be completed in a single tool call sequence.
- Each task must have a clear, verifiable completion criterion.
- MACHINE-READABILITY (2540A incident — the implement gate CANNOT verify prose):
  EVERY task MUST declare at least one backtick artifact path (e.g.
  `src/foo/bar.ts` under Expected Files) that the expected-files extractor
  accepts. A recognized validation command in backticks (e.g. `npx tsc --noEmit`)
  is additional proof, never the scope itself. Prose like "Typecheck passes"
  WITHOUT a backtick path does NOT count. Documental tasks (title containing
  "doc") remain exempt from the code-path requirement, but must still name the
  document they write.
- The task list must cover 100% of requirements.md as designed in design.md.
- **Proportional task count.** Create the MINIMUM number of tasks that covers every FR exactly once. Do not split one FR into several tasks unless each split has its own independently verifiable outcome (a distinct file or command). A one-FR spec normally needs 1–2 tasks (implement + test); a task whose only purpose is to restate or "review" another task is padding and must be removed.
- Tasks must be ordered correctly — no task can depend on a later task.
- Mark ALL tasks as `[ ] pending` initially.

